e:T420,The distinction between acquired (implicit) and studied (explicit) knowledge has been explored extensively in second language acquisition research. <a href="https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Krashen's Principles and Practice in Second Language Acquisition (1982)</a> introduced the acquisition-learning distinction that underpins this article, and neurolinguistic research by <a href="https://benjamins.com/catalog/sibil.40" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paradis (2009)</a> has provided evidence that these two types of knowledge are stored in separate brain systems. The strong claim that explicit knowledge cannot become implicit knowledge is debated — <a href="https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.4324/9780203628942-15" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">skill acquisition theory (DeKeyser, 2007) (a single chapter in this book)</a> argues that it can, through extensive practice — but the core point holds: explicit study alone isn't enough.1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"phase-0--0b-acquisition-vs-study","kind":"phase-article","route":"/roadmap/phase-0/0b-acquisition-vs-study","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/phase-0/0b-acquisition-vs-study","sourceHash":"37eeba64bbcd5119837c826aa54525da6c9c57d6f03e9e305e525ad781acce5d","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T23:11:31.883Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"0B — Acquisition vs Study","zh":"0B — 语言习得与学习"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"There are two ways to develop language ability: acquisition and studying.","zh":"发展语言能力有两种方式：语言习得和学习。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Acquiring</strong> a language means building an intuitive knowledge of it. Acquisition is how we all learned our native language. Most of us don't know the grammar rules of our native language — we just know what sounds right and what doesn't. When speaking, we don't think about grammar or vocabulary. We think about the meaning we want to convey and the words flow naturally.","zh":"<strong>习得</strong>一门语言，意味着建立对这门语言的直觉性认知。我们所有人都是通过语言习得学会母语的。我们大多数人并不知道母语的语法规则——我们只是知道什么听起来对，什么听起来不对。说话时，我们不会思考语法或词汇，而是想着想要表达的意思，词语便会自然流淌出来。"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"For example, if you're fluent in English, you'd agree that <em>\"the big red dog\"</em> sounds more natural than <em>\"the red big dog,\"</em> but you probably aren't aware of the complicated grammar rule of adjective order that makes it so.","zh":"例如，如果你的英语很流利，你会同意<em>“the big red dog”</em>听起来比<em>“the red big dog”</em>更自然，但你可能并不知道，背后起作用的是复杂的形容词顺序语法规则。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Studying</strong> a language, on the other hand, is the act of learning <em>about</em> the language. Most language learning products focus on this \"skill-building\" approach — teaching you vocabulary and grammar as building blocks to construct sentences, a lot like a Madlibs game. Using this method, you translate your thoughts from your native language into your target language.","zh":"另一方面，<strong>学习</strong>一门语言，是指学习<em>关于</em>这门语言的知识。大多数语言学习产品都侧重于这种“技能构建”方式——把词汇和语法作为构造句子的积木，很像 Mad Libs 游戏。使用这种方法时，你会把自己的想法从母语翻译成目标语言。"}},{"id":"segment-0005","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>No amount of skill-building alone will make you fluent.</strong>","zh":"<strong>仅靠技能构建，无论投入多少，都不会让你流利。</strong>"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This may seem counter-intuitive. After all, skill-building is how you learned every other subject in school. Language is not like other subjects, however.","zh":"这听起来可能有些反直觉。毕竟，技能构建正是你在学校学习其他科目的方式。然而，语言并不像其他学科。"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"What sounds \"right\" or \"wrong\" in a language often doesn't follow predictable rules. You might be able to translate a phrase word-for-word from English to Korean, but it probably won't make sense. Even if it does, it won't sound natural. Languages are highly specific in unpredictable ways. It's impossible to hold every grammar rule and language-specific phrasing in your head while trying to have a conversation.","zh":"一门语言中什么听起来“对”或“不对”，往往并不遵循可预测的规则。你也许能把一个短语从英语逐字翻译成韩语，但译文很可能毫无意义。即使有意义，也不会听起来自然。语言在许多方面都具有难以预测的特殊性。想要进行对话时，不可能把所有语法规则和语言特有的表达方式都同时记在脑中。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Fortunately, your brain has a built-in mechanism for exactly this problem. Every one of us was born with the ability to naturally acquire language. Contrary to popular belief, this ability does not disappear when you become an adult. In fact, by pairing your mature analytical mind with your innate language acquisition ability, you can learn languages <em>faster</em> than children.","zh":"幸运的是，你的大脑内置了一个专门解决这个问题的机制。我们每个人天生都具备自然习得语言的能力。与普遍看法相反，这种能力并不会在成年后消失。事实上，将成熟的分析思维与天生的语言习得能力结合起来，你学语言的速度可以<em>快于</em>儿童。"}},{"id":"how-children-acquire-language","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"How Children Acquire Language","zh":"儿童如何习得语言"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Our brains are pattern recognition machines. This ability allows us to recognize the faces of other people, play chess, appreciate art or music, and — yes — learn languages.","zh":"我们的大脑是模式识别机器。这种能力让我们能够辨认他人的面孔、下棋、欣赏艺术或音乐，以及——没错——学习语言。"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Children learn language through exposure from their parents. Parents interact with their children, narrate their experiences, and often use simpler speech with added gestures: <a href=\"/roadmap/library/modified-input\">Modified Input</a>","zh":"儿童通过接触父母的语言来学习。父母会与孩子互动，讲述他们的经历，并经常配合手势，使用更简单的语言：<a href=\"/roadmap/library/modified-input\">调整后的输入</a>"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"blockquote","content":{"en":"<p><em>Uh oh! The ball rolled off the table. Are you hot? Here, let's take off your sweater. Do you want the red one or the blue one?</em></p>","zh":"<p><em>哎呀！球从桌子上滚下去了。你热吗？来，我们把毛衣脱下来。你想要红色的还是蓝色的？</em></p>"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Children acquire language because they understand the meaning behind the message. They listen to the world around them and desperately want to know what the adults are talking about and join the social group.","zh":"儿童之所以能习得语言，是因为他们理解信息背后的含义。他们倾听周围的世界，迫切想知道大人在谈论什么，并加入这个社群。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Over months and years of continuously hearing their parents, teachers, and relatives speak to them, their little brains decode the common patterns and they learn to speak like a native, without apparent effort.","zh":"在数月乃至数年持续听父母、老师和亲戚对他们说话的过程中，他们的小脑袋会解码常见模式，并且毫不费力地学会像母语者一样说话。"}},{"id":"replicating-acquisition-as-an-adult","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Replicating Acquisition as an Adult","zh":"成年后复现语言习得"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"As adults, we don't have a parent to talk to us 24/7, but we do have access to the entire internet. Using widely available media, we can emulate the language acquisition process — with some important differences.","zh":"成年后，我们没有父母全天候和我们说话，但我们可以接触到整个互联网。借助随处可得的媒体，我们可以模拟语言习得过程——当然，其中存在一些重要差异。"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The most obvious one is that you aren't a toddler. It's unreasonable to spend 14 hours per day being cared for by a native speaker who is constantly giving you level-appropriate language to listen to.","zh":"最明显的一点是，你不是幼儿。每天花 14 个小时由母语者照料，并不断接受适合你水平的语言输入，这并不现实。"}},{"id":"segment-0019","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You also already speak at least one language natively. This does affect your learning, but it can be used to your benefit. Babies don't have the option of looking up new words in a dictionary or referencing a textbook. You can.","zh":"此外，你已经至少能流利地说一门语言。这确实会影响你的学习，但也可以为你所用。婴儿无法选择查词典或参考教材来查找新词，而你可以。"}},{"id":"segment-0020","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"And finally, you're smarter than a 2-year-old. You can leverage your adult knowledge to learn much more quickly with the help of modern tools, the ability to read, and intentional study.","zh":"最后，你比两岁的孩子更聪明。借助现代工具、阅读能力和有意识的学习，你可以利用成年人的知识，更快地学习。"}},{"id":"segment-0021","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"You won't learn exactly like a child, but throughout your journey, you'll replicate many of the same processes that you used to learn your native language.","zh":"你不会完全像儿童那样学习，但在整个学习过程中，你会复现许多曾用于学习母语的相同过程。"}},{"id":"acquisition-and-study-together","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Acquisition and Study Together","zh":"语言习得与学习相结合"}},{"id":"segment-0023","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The Refold method uses <em>both</em> acquisition and study — but in the right balance. Study (what we often call \"priming\") prepares you to understand things in the language. You leverage your adult brain to take shortcuts and learn more quickly. But then acquisition (through immersion) is where the real learning happens. Study supports acquisition, but can never replace it. <a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">The Pillars of Language Learning</a>","zh":"Refold 方法同时采用<em>语言习得</em>和学习——但会保持恰当的平衡。学习（我们经常称之为“预热”）能帮助你做好理解语言内容的准备。你可以利用成年人的大脑走捷径，从而更快地学习。但真正的学习发生在语言习得（通过沉浸）过程中。学习可以支持语言习得，却永远无法取代它。<a href=\"/roadmap/library/the-pillars-of-language-learning\">语言学习的支柱</a>"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与论证"}},{"id":"segment-0025","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"$e","zh":"二语习得研究广泛探讨了习得的（隐性的）知识与学习的（显性的）知识之间的区别。<a href=\"https://www.sdkrashen.com/content/books/principles_and_practice.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Krashen 的《第二语言习得的原则与实践》（1982）</a>提出了支撑本文的“习得—学习”区别；而<a href=\"https://benjamins.com/catalog/sibil.40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Paradis（2009）</a>的神经语言学研究则提供了证据，表明这两类知识储存在不同的大脑系统中。关于显性知识无法转化为隐性知识这一强主张，目前仍存在争议——<a href=\"https://www.taylorfrancis.com/chapters/edit/10.4324/9780203628942-15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">技能习得理论（DeKeyser，2007）（该书中的单独一章）</a>认为，通过大量练习，这种转化是可能的——但核心观点依然成立：仅靠显性学习是不够的。"}},{"id":"segment-0026","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The claim that adults can acquire language like children is supported by <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0010027718300994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hartshorne, Tenenbaum &amp; Pinker (2018)</a>, who found that the \"critical period\" for language learning extends much later than previously thought and that decline is gradual, not sudden. <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2307/3588095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Marinova-Todd, Marshall &amp; Snow (2000)</a> addressed common misconceptions about age and language learning directly. Adults may have slight disadvantages in ultimate phonological attainment, but have significant advantages in rate of learning due to cognitive maturity, literacy, and metacognition.","zh":"成年人能够像儿童一样习得语言这一观点，得到了<a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0010027718300994\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Hartshorne、Tenenbaum 和 Pinker（2018）</a>研究的支持。他们发现，语言学习的“关键期”比以往认为的延续得更久，能力衰退是渐进的，而非突然发生的。<a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.2307/3588095\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Marinova-Todd、Marshall 和 Snow（2000）</a>直接回应了关于年龄与语言学习的常见误解。成年人在最终的语音掌握程度上可能略有劣势，但凭借认知成熟度、读写能力和元认知能力，在学习速度上具有显著优势。"}},{"id":"segment-0027","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The idea that our brains are pattern recognition machines that decode language statistically is supported by <a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.274.5294.1926\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Saffran, Aslin &amp; Newport (1996)</a>, who demonstrated statistical learning in 8-month-old infants, and <a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3720232/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Batterink &amp; Neville (2013)</a>, who showed that the brain processes syntax even without conscious awareness.","zh":"我们的大脑是模式识别机器，会通过统计方式解码语言；这一观点得到了<a href=\"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.274.5294.1926\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Saffran、Aslin 和 Newport（1996）</a>研究的支持，他们展示了 8 个月大婴儿的统计学习能力；<a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3720232/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Batterink 和 Neville（2013）</a>则表明，即使没有意识到语法，大脑也会处理句法。"}},{"id":"segment-0028","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The effectiveness of combining explicit study with immersion — rather than relying on either alone — is well-supported by the form-focused instruction literature, including <a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1362168816628627\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ellis (2016)</a> and <a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1944-9720.2003.tb02123.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wong &amp; VanPatten (2003)</a>, who showed that traditional grammar drills are far less effective than meaning-focused activity paired with brief form-focused attention.","zh":"将显性学习与沉浸相结合，而不是只依赖其中一种方式，其有效性得到了形式聚焦教学研究的大力支持，包括<a href=\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1362168816628627\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Ellis（2016）</a>以及<a href=\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1944-9720.2003.tb02123.x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wong 和 VanPatten（2003）</a>的研究。他们表明，传统语法操练的效果远不如以意义为中心、辅以短暂形式关注的活动。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
